-- L1 step-2b's discriminating repro (sprint/ctor-identity,
-- S-ctor-oracle-identity-family, L1-oracle-typectors-key-identity), promoted to a
-- durable multi-module `analyze_project` fixture.  Two modules declare a
-- same-named type `T` with DIFFERENT constructor sets; the scrutinee's type
-- arrives through a FUNCTION (`zzt.mk`) so the *bystander* (`aat`) is the
-- name-IMPORTED module and `zzt` -- the type `mk` actually returns -- is only
-- bare-imported.  A THIRD module (`byw`) participates in nothing and must stay
-- clean, proving this is not merely "the graph happens to be small".
--
-- EXPECTED RESULT, DERIVED BY HAND BEFORE ANY RUN (this corpus has NO
-- regeneration script; oracle.json is hand-written and reviewed):
--   `mk n : zzt.T`, whose constructors are exactly `X`, `Y`, `Z`; all three
--   have arms below, so `g` is EXHAUSTIVE.  `aat.T`'s constructors `A`/`B` are
--   never in scope for this match at all -- `T`'s value here can only mean
--   `zzt.T`.  `byw.Widget` is used only as an ordinary value, matched by
--   nothing.  Every file in the graph publishes ZERO diagnostics.
--
-- MEASURED AT BASE (`c7c879b7`, before this family): the oracle instead
-- answered from `aat`'s row (loaded first) and demanded `A`:
--   "Warning: non-exhaustive match of 'T'. Missing case: 'A'; add a 'A => ...'
--   arm, or a '_' wildcard arm to catch the rest."  Swapping the two import
--   lines flipped the same program to clean -- order-dependent at base.
--   (`S-ctor-oracle-identity-family-implementer.md`, L1 step 2b-ii.)
import aat.{T(..)}
import zzt.{X, Y, Z, mk}
import byw.{Widget, defaultWidget}

g : Int -> Int
g n = match mk n
  X => 1
  Y => 2
  Z => 3

w : Widget
w = defaultWidget

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = println (g 0)
  let _ = w
  println "ok"
